7592186129326 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129326 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129326 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e669e123-e445-550d-980b-09ee4b440f81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130016 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130016 #edb/ref 1061 22162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130016 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5af3abd2-cf0a-5230-8fd4-5f285e096712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130016 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130017 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130017 #edb/ref 1061 22163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130017 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c4da688-00e6-5efe-81b1-0c1749b7c89f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130017 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130018 #edb/ref 1061 22164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130018 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27f830a8-2df2-594e-9bbe-3c8973052195" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130018 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130019 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130019 #edb/ref 1061 22180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130019 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b58904dd-f0af-56ab-937b-9eafb5f01b0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130019 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130370 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the following function: \n\n$$\nw = f(x,y,z) =x^2 +y^2+z^2\n$$\n\nSadly, it is impossible to plot this function because four spatial dimensions ($x,y,z,$ and $w$) are needed, and space as we know it is restricted to three dimensions. \n\nHowever, we can get an idea of how the function $f(x,y,z)$ behaves by plotting surfaces of the form\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3 \\, : \\,f(x,y,z)=c\\right\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is some value that is in the range of $f.$ These sets are called **level surfaces** because they can be visualized as surfaces in three-dimensional space, and are \"level\" in the sense that each surface corresponds to a constant value of $w.$\n\nFor this particular function, all level surfaces are spheres. For example, if we let $w = 4,$ we get\n\n$$\nx^2 +y^2+z^2 = 4,\n$$\n\nwhich is a sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/12/12de396a8460b929f656a44d701752e248180f926f22a5f5c3eeecf355e9f31a.png)\n\nFunctions with more than two input variables are sometimes called **hypersurfaces** because they can be thought of as surfaces that exist within a space whose dimension is greater than $3.$ For example, the function \n\n$$\nw = x^2+y^2+z^2\n$$\n\ncan be thought of as a sphere in four-dimensional space, and is sometimes called a **hypersphere.**\n\nAnother way of thinking about the level surfaces of this function is that they correspond to the intersection of the hypersphere $w=x^2+y^2+z^2$ and the **hyperplane** $w=c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130370 #edb/ref 1065 3818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130370 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0682086d-c964-557f-930b-8d2c1ebfd879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130370 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259949 #edb/ref 1080 "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259949 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259949 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07f39980-6ad0-53d7-ad63-fbb34a382f5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260057 #edb/ref 1080 "A family of elliptic cones: ${z}^{2}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260057 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260057 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1456f86c-f369-5c8a-aa73-f917e987fcd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260745 #edb/ref 1080 "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+C$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260745 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260745 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "64d0fc97-78ec-5077-acfa-96dd790ebb7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261222 #edb/ref 1080 "A family of planes: $z=x+3y+C$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261222 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261222 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9b989c09-44c2-5eab-8bb7-5a6b7ceb875f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261283 #edb/ref 1080 "A family of hyperbolic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+C$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261283 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261283 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a1c9051c-074f-5a24-a621-b2a3449ab493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262296 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186259949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262296 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262296 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262296 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262296 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262296 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262296 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262296 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186259949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262296 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "653d007f-928b-501b-88fc-8c02a0e38f08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262977 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+z+3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9849d588-2a95-5d11-993e-dd1a8d5d4837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262977 #edb/ref 1074 "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\