ref 17592186045417] [17592186338506 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338506 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186337815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338506 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8165f0ea-43de-5434-a42c-9e10144a7415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338966 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338966 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338966 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338966 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=z-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point $P(2,1,22).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338966 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e38a7de1-ef3c-50e7-9539-8728abb34f00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338966 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1,22) & =22-4(2)^{2}-6(1)^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ z & =4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(2,1,22)$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
